artifact.self_contained_problem:
Consider a protoplanetary "transition disc," i.e., a circumstellar accretion disc around a young pre-main-sequence star whose continuum emission at millimeter wavelengths exhibits a resolved central cavity (a region of strongly reduced dust surface density extending from the star out to a cavity-edge radius R_cav of order tens of astronomical units), while the disc beyond R_cav remains millimeter-bright. Such "mm-bright" transition discs are observed to retain detectable gas (e.g., CO isotopologue line emission, near-infrared CO ro-vibrational lines, and accretion signatures onto the central star) inside the dust cavity, but spatially resolved sub-millimeter line observations also indicate that the gas surface density Σ_gas(r) inside R_cav is reduced relative to a smooth extrapolation of the outer-disc profile by factors typically in the range 10–10^4, with the magnitude and radial shape of the drop varying among sources and tracers.

Provide a single, internally consistent physical model of the gas component inside the dust cavities of mm-bright transition discs that simultaneously specifies:

(a) the radial profile of the gas surface density Σ_gas(r) for r < R_cav, including the location, depth, and width of any gas density drop(s);
(b) the gas temperature structure T_gas(r,z) and ionization/chemical state determining the abundances of the observed tracers (CO and its isotopologues, atomic and ionized species seen in UV/optical/IR);
(c) the gas kinematics inside the cavity, including the deviation (if any) from Keplerian rotation, the presence of radial flows (inflow/accretion streams or outflows/winds), and any non-axisymmetric components;
(d) the physical mechanism (or combination of mechanisms — e.g., dynamical clearing by one or more embedded planets, photoevaporative winds, magnetohydrodynamic disc winds, dead-zone dust trapping, grain growth) responsible for producing this structure;

and that quantitatively reconciles the apparent tension between (i) the inferred large drops in gas surface density inside the cavity and (ii) the simultaneous detections of gas (in molecular line emission, scattered-light tracers, and ongoing stellar accretion) within the same cavities.

